{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#139","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#139","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#139\",\"parent_revision\":\"50091f27dc9bda230c995725b6585d889782ce0db88d9c2a6333524a9cdfde19\",\"root_revision\":\"50091f27dc9bda230c995725b6585d889782ce0db88d9c2a6333524a9cdfde19\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nTwo codes, same claim:\\n\\n- Pydantic's `max_length` yields **FM005** \u2014 \\\"Exceeds maximum length of 64\\\"\\n- `check_fm010` yields **FM010** \u2014 \\\"Name must be 1-64 characters\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Fix Has A Side Effect Worth Weighing\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRemoving `max_length` from the boundary models makes FM010 the sole owner and, as a side effect, lets `fix_skill_name_field` run without `NameFormatValidator`.\\n\\nBut it changes what the boundary models reject for **every** consumer, not just this rule \u2014 so it is a typed-boundary decision, not a rule tweak.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- #102 \u2014 where this was found\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e2f8ff9901756615234abe2b90151703ff9fc42d9ccc4ca6f21c15b5dd6628f1\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDORXxKvc8AAAABXP-d_g\"}"}